plet already (can become kong if you draw 4th T6).  \n- Overall hand is unbalanced across suits; you could aim for Half Flush (Bamboo + honors) or mixed hand.\n\nBest discards are tiles that are isolated, not helping your sequences/pungs, and safe given discards so far.\n\n**Reason:**  \nFrom safety viewpoint:  \n- Honors (F2 gone, F1/F3/F4 scarce) are risky to discard now if drawn later.  \n- Terminals like B9/T9/B8 scarce; but you don\u2019t hold them.  \n- Considering opponent\u2019s melds: next player has W6,W7,W8 Chow and F3 pung, so discarding W3/W4 might be safer (they already used W6-W8, so W3/W4 less likely to feed Chow).  \n- Your hand\u2019s strongest assets are T6 triplet and T4,T5,T6 sequence potential. Weakest tiles are singles without nearby connectors: B7 (only B5 distant), B5 (only B4 nearby), W4 (only W3 nearby but W3 is pair, so W4 less useful).  \n\nDiscarding **B7** seems reasonable:  \n- B7 is single, no immediate sequence/pung potential in hand (B5 present but B6 missing, B8/B9 missing).  \n- B7 unseen count is 3, so moderate danger but less than discarding a wind/tile others might need for pung.  \n- It doesn\u2019t break your potential melds; you keep T6 triplet, T4,T5,T6 sequence, B4 pair, W3 pair.  \n- Moves you toward reducing Bamboo clutter, possibly aiming for Mixed Hand or All Types later.\n\n**Answer:** Play B7"}
