g disqualifies Half Flush unless I change plan.  \n- Alternative: \"All Types\" (W, T, B, F, J) - I have W, T, B (B4 pung), and could draw F or J for honors. Current hand: W, T, B present; need F and J in meld/pair. That might be achievable.  \n\nConsidering discards:  \n- Play T4: T4 only 1 left, safe? T4 has been played by next player and across, likely safe. But T4 is Bamboo, I have many Bamboos, keeping T4 might help with Bamboo chows.  \n- Play W2, W3, W6, W7: These are Characters, less useful if aiming for All Types or Bamboo focus, but they could form chows. W3 maybe riskier since W3 has 3 left but next player has W6-7-8 chow, previous has W5-6-7 chow, so W3 might be safe.  \n- Play T7: T7 has 2 left, could be useful for chow T5-6-7 or pung.  \n- Play T5: T5 abundant (4 left), good to keep for flexibility.  \n- Play T6: I have T6x3, almost a pung, discarding T6 would break that potential, unwise.  \n\nBest discard: W2 or W7, as they are isolated Characters with few prospects given tile availability. W2 has 2 left, but no W1 (0) for chow, W4(1) scarce. W7 has 1 left, but W6(1) and W8(1) scarce, chow unlikely. Discarding W7 might be slightly safer since W7 has been played by me and others in history.  \n\nReason: I aim to develop a hand with scoring potential, possibly \"All Types\" or \"All Pungs\" by focusing on Bamboo pungs/chows and drawing honors. Discarding a Character tile reduces suit clutter and avoids dangerous discards (like honors or terminals). W7 is less connected and has fewer remaining tiles for improvement.  \n\nAnswer: Play W7"}
